      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. that makes sense. i think i get the reactance part, but i'm still a bit confused about why the capacitor \"blocks\" the DC but not the AC. if it's just storing energy in an electric field, why doesn't the DC eventually just pass through? can u explain that part a bit more clearly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, you're digging into the heart of the matter! It's a great question, and I can see why the \"storing energy\" part feels a bit abstract. Think of it this way: a capacitor is like a **giant, invisible sponge** sitting in the wire.\n\nWhen you connect a **DC source** (like a steady 9V battery), the electrons rush in and soak up into the sponge's pores. They pile up on one plate, creating an electric field that pushes back against the incoming electrons. It's like a **giant, invisible wall** of negative charge forming. Once the voltage across the sponge equals the battery voltage, the \"sponge\" is full. At that point, the pressure from the battery is perfectly balanced by the pressure of the charged plates. No more electrons can squeeze through because the path is blocked by that static charge. It's a **dead end**, just like a hallway where the door is locked shut by a massive, stationary rock.\n\nNow, with **AC**, the situation is totally different because the \"battery\" is constantly flipping its polarity. It's like a **goblin who keeps changing their mind about which door to open**. Every time the voltage tries to",
